{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction to the previous comment: the backfill does not trip the spend cap\n\nThe previous comment asserted the corpus backfill is \"precisely the large embedding or small-model batch job shape the cap README warns about\" and framed it as an availability risk to the merge gate. That was an unquantified inference. Measured against the actual rates, it is wrong on magnitude.\n\n**The cap's parameters are in-repo**, not operator-local: `infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf:31-52` defaults `bedrock_daily_cap_usd = 500` and `bedrock_warn_threshold_usd = 300`. The metering ceiling is `handler.py:110-115` — `InputTokenCount` 6.875e-06 USD/token, `OutputTokenCount` 3.4375e-05, each 1.25x the highest per-token rate the account was billed over a trailing 60 days (Claude Opus binds every direction).\n\n**Backfill priced at those ceilings**, using ADR 0122's measured per-ticket counts (digest input ~1,260, digest output ~300, embedding input ~300, measured over its 630-ticket corpus):\n\n| scope | n | input tokens | output tokens | metered | of $500 cap | of $300 warn |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ADR benchmark corpus | 630 | 982,800 | 189,000 | $13.25 | 2.7% | 4.4% |\n| live store | 5,663 | 8,834,280 | 1,698,900 | **$119.14** | 23.8% | 39.7% |\n| all ticket dirs | 9,780 | 15,256,800 | 2,934,000 | $205.75 | 41.1% | 68.6% |\n\nA full live-store backfill meters at **$119.14 — under a quarter of the hard cap and under 40% of the warn threshold**. Even pricing every ticket directory rather than live tickets only reaches $205.75, still below the warn line. The backfill does not trip the cap on its own, and the merge-gate availability risk described in the previous comment is not supported.\n\nTwo refinements that survive:\n\n- **$119 is an upper bound, and a loose one.** The ceiling rates are Opus-derived with 1.25x headroom while the backfill runs entirely on Haiku and Titan, which the cap README puts up to two orders of magnitude below the ceiling. Real billed cost is far under the metered figure. This is the over-pricing the README describes, operating exactly as designed.\n- **The residual exposure is additive, not standalone.** The cap is a daily account-wide total shared with the review-bot's `LLM-Review` traffic, so the backfill's ~$119 lands on top of that day's existing spend. The variable's own description calls the cap \"deliberately far above normal usage... a runaway guard, not a throttle for routine work\", so the headroom is expected to absorb it.\n\n**Unverified:** this account's actual normal daily Bedrock spend. The cap README states the measured distribution is deliberately kept out of this repository because it is public, so the margin between normal traffic and the $300 warn line cannot be checked from the tree.\n\n**Net effect on the child's prerequisites.** The ADR's named prerequisite stands unchanged and remains genuinely blocking: `iam_s7.tf` admits only `anthropic.claude-*`, so the Titan embedding call is denied in any deployed environment until that resource list is widened. The spend cap is **not** a second blocking prerequisite — it is a bounded consumption worth scheduling deliberately (run the one-time backfill on a low-traffic day, or use the `vector-raw` variant, which drops the Haiku digest and meters at $49.06), not a gate to clear.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793546097358434846,"uuid":"3dd9d05d-bdd1-4ace-b781-57bd5f5847df"}